Z||000004||Ferrari355||02-21-2000||04:35 PM||||Ok, well i can give alittle more info now that i've calmed down abit. Well it is a 87 GT V6, auto. Power Windoes and locks. sunroof. IT ha a morose sway bar that he installed on 98. It is in really good shape except has high miles and the A/C doesn't work because like the AC cable or sometihng is by the Firewall and gets hot or something. But he is asking $3500 but he will take $2500 for it so thats what we will give him. We are going to take it for a long drive when we test drive it to make sure it has no major problems. If you have any other questions just e-mail me. Thanks<BR>Alex... PS, since most of you have Fiero, can yuo tell me things to check when we look like specific things. thanks||||reg||12
Z||000005||Old Lar||02-21-2000||05:08 PM||||Looks like mine,($16K when new) which I bought new in '87.  Depending on the cars historical care, it can be a great driver or a money pit.  A high milage 13 year old car, will probably need lots of stuff like hoses, water pump, belts, A/C fix (I spent $800 for the repair to keep the freon stuff) I spent $400 to convert to the 134a coolant on my '88. A high milage 2.8 V-6 is looking at an overhaul in the near future.  Look at the CV boots, wheel bearings, brakes (emergency brake cable), catalytic converter,$100, sensors O2, etc.  Make sure there is no frame rust. The coolant tubes corrode at the support brackets: two brackets on each tube.  Some of the stuff may have already been done. <P>My '87 has low milage for a 13 year old car &lt;75K and has been basically trouble free. I over maintain it.  My '88 has been a money pit,(bought used)and in 3 years the suspension was replaced, steering rack, engine, transmission, fuel rail, fuel pump(x2), new wheels, tires, K/N air filter, plugs, wires (x2), A/C compressor, A/C lines, rebuilt radio, new front speakers, complete repaint, cat back Borla exhaust, brakes, you name it, its been repaced or repaired. <P>The Fiero drives nice, the body panels won't rust, the car looks good  and is a blast to drive. Keep in mind "ITS A 13 YEAR OLD CAR"!  If you are talented and enjoy working on a Fiero, you can do most of the work yourself.  Just don't go fixing up the car real nice and tap a tree or pole hot dogging around town.  There are lots of part sources out there.||||reg||10
Z||000006||87GT||02-21-2000||05:58 PM||||Based on the experience I have had with my 87GT, I would check for any cracks in the exhaust manifold.  Mine had a slight tick when I bought it and played it off until it was so loud that I had to replace it.  Nothing worse than a loud ticking car, makes it sound like a heap of junk. lol||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||02-21-2000||09:27 PM||||Hey Alex. Nice looking car. As far as the rust problem goes, since it is an Okie car, shouldn't be a problem. I would be more concerned about the electricals and the a/c probs.  You and your dad may want to take a spare box of fuses on the test drive and take a good look at the fuse panel. The odometer in my car stopped when I blew the radio fuse. Speedometer stopped too. changed the fuse and right as rain again.  But after seeing what $2500 buys in OKas far as V6 Fieros go, seems like a good deal. May I make a suggestion though?  If you buy it, spend some money on an engine stand and a junkyard motor and start building it up.  Look forward to seeing the car at the Fiero Club of Oklahoma car show in Norman May 20th! <p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
